<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/expert/social-theory-marx-to-bourdieu">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social theory is sociology's think-engine: <strong>the frameworks that decide what counts as
    an explanation</strong>. The founders — Marx (conflict), Durkheim (structure), Weber
    (meaning) — still organise every question in the field, and the twentieth-century extensions
    (Bourdieu, Goffman, Foucault, intersectionality) turned their insights into precision tools.
    This chapter reads the grand theories through a specific problem: identity. Where does the
    self come from, how does society hold it in place, and what forces shape which self you get?
    Each theorist is an answer machine for one of those questions, and the unit's three topics —
    learning identity, social control, the influences on social identity — map almost perfectly
    onto the theory's three generations.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founders, their core moves, and the modern toolbox" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />

  <h2>How do we learn our identity? Socialisation and habitus</h2>
  <p>
    The classical answer is socialisation: primary socialisation in the family (language, norms,
    emotional defaults), secondary socialisation through school, peers and work, lifelong after
    that. George Herbert Mead gave it a mechanism: the self emerges by taking the attitude of
    others toward yourself — the play stage (significant others), the game stage (the
    "generalised other", society's standpoint internalised), until the "I" (the spontaneous
    actor) and the "me" (the internalised social expectation) form a dialogue. Cooley's
    looking-glass self compressed it: we imagine how we appear to others, imagine their judgment,
    and adjust. Marx's answer is colder and prior to all of this: the class position you are born
    into shapes what there is to learn. Bourdieu supplied the synthesis — <strong>habitus</strong>,
    the set of durable dispositions (tastes, postures, sense of what is "for people like us")
    acquired through practical experience of a class position, operating without explicit
    teaching. In <em>Distinction</em> (1979), survey data showed musical, artistic and food
    preferences mapping onto class fractions with uncomfortable precision: taste is not a
    personality trait but class incorporated into the body. Goffman adds the performance layer —
    front stage and back stage — the self continuously edited for its audience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Marx's structural priority, spelled out.</strong> Before any socialisation happens,
    the Marxist claim runs, you have been assigned a class position, and the mechanisms run
    deeper than income. <em>Alienation</em> has four faces in the 1844 Manuscripts: from the
    product of your work, from the activity itself, from other workers, and from your own
    species-capacities. <em>Commodity fetishism</em> (Capital, volume one) explains why the
    system is invisible from inside: social relations between people appear as relations
    between things — prices seem like properties of objects rather than crystallised labour.
    <em>Class consciousness</em> is the variable the whole theory turns on: a class "in
    itself" (sharing a position) becomes "for itself" (knowing it, organising) only through
    struggle — which is why the gig economy is such a rich test case: workers sharing a
    platform, algorithmically supervised, individually isolated. Whether the shared position
    generates shared consciousness without shared workplaces is Marx's mechanism under live
    observation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bourdieu, worked.</strong> His capital conversions are the mechanism to remember:
    a family converts economic capital into cultural capital by buying piano lessons and
    bookshelves (and the ease, the "natural" fluency, that goes with them); the cultural
    capital converts back into economic capital at university-admission and job-interview
    stages, where fluency reads as merit. The surveys of <em>Distinction</em> made this
    measurable — taste in music, sport and food mapping onto class position with startling
    regularity — and the education-based inheritance studies that followed (children of
    graduates reading to their children more, applying differently, knowing which subjects
    "are for people like us") operationalise habitus for every generation of researchers
    since. The uncomfortable summary: meritocracy's measurement instruments partly measure
    inherited advantage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "habitus is just another word for personality." Personality
    is individual and varies within classes; habitus is class-shared disposition — the reason
    people from the same background feel "at home" in some institutions and "not for the likes
    of us" in others without ever being told. Bourdieu's point is that inequality reproduces
    itself silently through comfort and discomfort.
  </p>

  <h2>How does society control us? From anomie to the panopticon</h2>
  <p>
    Durkheim's answer established sociology itself: <em>social facts</em> — norms, laws, rates —
    are real things above individuals that constrain them. His <em>Suicide</em> (1897) is still
    the template demonstration: suicide rates are stable year to year within a society and vary
    systematically between groups (higher among the less integrated — his "egoistic" type —
    Protestants than Catholics in nineteenth-century Europe), so even the most private act has
    social patterning. Control fails when regulation collapses — <em>anomie</em>, the normless
    state of sudden change. Weber's answer runs through legitimacy: we obey because authority is
    believed — traditional, charismatic, or rational-legal (rules, offices, the bureaucrat's
    stamp) — and rational-legal control's dark side is the <em>iron cage</em>: life organised,
    timed and administered. Foucault radicalised it: modern power disciplines through
    surveillance and normalisation, with the panopticon (Bentham's prison design — visible
    unverifiable watching) as the diagram; you internalise the watcher, and control becomes
    self-administered. Marx's version is material: ideology — the ruling ideas of each age being
    the ideas of its ruling class — makes existing arrangements feel natural. Three mechanisms,
    not rivals: norms, legitimacy and internalised surveillance interlock.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The informal layer.</strong> Most social control never involves the state at all:
    shame, gossip, exclusion, side-eye. Anthropologists found small-scale societies running
    almost entirely on such sanctions — ridicule policing would-be big men, ostracism as the
    supreme penalty. Modern equivalents are the workplace peer-review, the group chat, the
    school corridor: reputational systems with real teeth. Foucault's insight names their
    modern upgrade — when surveillance is continuous and unverifiable, the watching moves
    inside, and the guard towers become unnecessary. The analytical point: formal control
    (police, courts, examination systems) is the visible fraction of a much larger informal
    machine, and the two interlock — law works because informal sanction backs it, and
    informal norms harden into law when they matter enough.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Durkheim claimed individuals never really cause their own
    suicides." He claimed the <em>rate</em> — a property of the group — is socially explainable
    even though each individual act has idiosyncratic causes. The move from individual case to
    social rate is the whole methodological point of sociology, and conflating the two levels is
    the standard beginner error. Nor did he deny agency — the theory explains the distribution
    of outcomes, not each individual's fate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "theory is armchair speculation — just bring the evidence."
    Every dataset is collected under a theory: Suicide's rates were found by looking where the
    theory said to look; a survey that never asks about meaning (Weber) or capital (Bourdieu)
    cannot find them. "Evidence first" is itself a positivist position — one lens among several,
    not the absence of lenses.
  </p>

  <h2>What influences our social identity? Class, status and intersection</h2>
  <p>
    Weber's three-dimensional stratification is the workhorse: <em>class</em> (market position —
    what you can sell), <em>status</em> (honour and lifestyle group), <em>party</em> (organised
    power) — and their frequent mismatch (the rich but low-status parvenu) explains politics
    that pure class analysis cannot. Bourdieu made capital multidimensional: economic (money),
    cultural (credentials, taste, embodied fluency) and social (networks, contacts) — with the
    crucial addition of <em>convertibility</em>: families convert economic capital into cultural
    capital through education, and cultural capital silently converts back into economic
    advantage in job markets. Gender, ethnicity and religion cross-cut all of it, and
    intersectionality (Crenshaw, 1989) names the analytical failure when they are treated one at
    a time: in <em>DeGraffenreid v. General Motors</em> (1976), Black women suing over seniority-
    based layoffs lost because the court saw discrimination claims only as race (which Black men
    could claim) or sex (which white women could claim) — the composite experience fit neither
    template. The lesson: social identity is multi-axis, and the axes interact non-additively.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "intersectionality just means oppressions stack — add racism
    to sexism and rank the sums." Crenshaw's claim was precisely that the disadvantage is
    <em>non</em>-additive: the DeGraffenreid plaintiffs were not harmed by "racism plus sexism"
    but by an intersection the law's separate categories could not represent at all. The
    analytical lesson runs through the whole stratification tradition — Weber's class, status
    and party, and Bourdieu's three capitals, are themselves multi-axis systems built because
    one-dimensional rankings mispredict both behaviour and politics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reference groups and social capital.</strong> Merton supplied the mechanism that
    makes identity inherently comparative: people orient to <em>reference groups</em> they do
    not yet belong to — the student dressing like the profession she means to enter is doing
    anticipatory socialisation — which is how aspiration itself becomes a social fact rather
    than a private mood. Bourdieu's social capital formalised the network half of the account:
    who you can call, who vouches for you, whose tables you can be seen at. Putnam's <em>Bowling
    Alone</em> (2000) made the aggregate case measurable — associational membership falling
    across postwar America — and split the concept usefully into <em>bonding</em> capital
    (in-group solidarity) and <em>bridging</em> capital (ties across groups): the first
    comforts, the second gets you jobs, and the two trade off. Identity research now has its
    full instrument set — position (class), honour (status), networks (social capital), embodied
    dispositions (habitus) — plus the axes of gender, ethnicity and religion cutting across
    every one of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Marx thought culture, law and religion are a mere puppet of
    the economy, so the theory is refuted by autonomous art." Marx and Engels allowed the
    superstructure "relative autonomy" and lagged effects (Engels says exactly this in his 1890s
    letters); the claim is that material production <em>conditions</em> the field of ideas, not
    that it dictates each item. Attacking the puppet version wins the argument against a theory
    nobody held.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading theory as a practitioner</h2>
  <p>
    The practical skill is running the lenses deliberately. A strike looks like class conflict
    (Marx), normative breakdown or re-solidification (Durkheim), or a contest over legitimacy and
    meaning (Weber) — all three analyses run, each revealing what the others miss, and choosing
    one is a research-design decision, not a mood. Bourdieu's concepts generate testable
    predictions (his cultural-capital measures became standard survey instruments; education
    research operationalises habitus as cultural-destination matching). Goffman's vocabulary
    escaped the academy — "front stage", "impression management" — because social media made
    dramaturgy everyone's daily practice. And theory debates are method debates: structure versus
    agency, macro versus micro, positivism versus interpretivism — every research design takes a
    side, usually silently. Learning the theory is learning what your methods can and cannot see.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The reproduction problem, formalised.</strong> Bourdieu and Passeron's
    <em>Reproduction</em> (1970) argued schooling converts social hierarchy into academic
    hierarchy while appearing meritocratic — because exams test culturally specific fluency as if
    it were talent. The empirical programme this spawned (cultural participation, elite-school
    admissions, the "effectively maintained inequality" literature) is one of sociology's most
    productive research lines; its open question is whether expansion of higher education
    dilutes the mechanism or merely displaces the competition to postgraduate credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Foucault after the internet.</strong> The panopticon assumed a scarcity of watchers;
    platforms inverted the economics of observation (Zuboff's "surveillance capitalism" is the
    standard framing). Whether Foucault's analytics of self-discipline still fit when observation
    is automated, indexed and monetised is a live debate — his tools (normalisation, docile
    bodies, the exam) partly fit and partly miss algorithmic control, which is exactly the kind
    of productive strain theory exists to generate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Is class consciousness returning (post-2008 inequality
    politics) or has identity displaced class as the axis of mobilisation — a false dichotomy?
    Can Bourdieu's framework survive globalisation's new capitals (attention, data)? And does
    intersectionality have a formal model yet, or is it a corrective discipline that resists
    one? Each is argued seriously in the current journals.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Marx, Capital (1867); Durkheim, Suicide (1897); Weber, The Protestant Ethic (1905); Bourdieu, Distinction (1979); Goffman, The Presentation of Self (1959); Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975).</p>
